  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/23/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_113.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Johnny rushes to meet up with Doris Evans, his date, who is astonished that he dared show up in his Human Torch uniform to escort her out.&nbsp; Despite repeated attempts to fly her, Doris has absolutely <i>no interest</i> in the Human Torch and insists they take the bus.&nbsp; She continually talks about him settling down and won't even allow Johnny to drive fast in his Corvette, much to his amazement -- yet for the first time, it seems this is a girl who genuinely prefers him as plain, ordinary Johnny Storm and is unfazed by his more glamorous identity as member of the Fantastic Four.\n\nJust before they leave, however, Johnny overhears her father arguing with their gardener (name given as only \"Sam\"), who is experimenting with increasing the IQ of plants with a strange device.&nbsp; It's too much for the older Evans, and he fires Sam on the spot.&nbsp; Johnny thinks the gardener is a creep but knows that it's none of his business.\n\nLater that night, a storm begins to brew, and Sam, the gardener is at home with his device, determined to show the world that he's no fool.&nbsp; Suddenly, his gadget is struck by lightning in a \"one-in-a-million freak accident,\" and he soon discovers he can give orders to plants <i>through</i> the device.&nbsp; The rays that emanate from it now have made him the \"master of all plant life,\" and he immediately dubs himself \"Plantman.\"&nbsp; His first thought is to \"avenge myself on the one who <i>fired</i> me!\"\n\nAn hour later, he breaks into the jewelry store that Doris Evans's father manages by instructing the plants within to deactivate the burglar alarm, unlock the front door, allowing him to slip in undetected.&nbsp; He then steals the money out of its safe, and \"plants\" evidence that Evans did it.&nbsp; Sure enough, police cars are soon seen outside the Evans house, and Johnny happens to see them as he drives by.&nbsp; He promises Doris that he'll clear her father's name.\n\nAnd indeed, the next night, as Johnny is flying patrol over Glenville, he spots the Plantman in front of another jewelry store but is soon surprised by his ability to control plants when they spray the Torch with dew and then bind his ankles with their roots, giving Plantman time to escape.\n\nThe next day, the Plantman arrives in Central Park, this time wearing a green costume that \"will symbolize my terrifying <i>power</i>!\"&nbsp; He declares that this will be his new headquarters and orders all the nearby plants to \"bow down to me!\"&nbsp; He then orders a guard to tell the mayor to surrender the city to him, or his plant allies shall tear down City Hall!&nbsp; He's actually shown thinking that first it's NYC, then \"city after city...and finally...<i>the world</i>!\" (because plants outnumber people).&nbsp; \"Today, Earth!&nbsp; Tomorrow, the <i>stars</i>!\"\n\nThe news of Plantman's demand is soon broadcast over the radio, and after learning that the new villain is in Central Park, the Human Torch goes after him.&nbsp; And after a final two and a half pages of a largely unimaginative struggle (which even includes the Plantman trying to upend a \"vat of water\" that is conveniently just sitting around the park), Johnny sends up enough heat to evaporate most of the moisture from the surrounding plants.\n\nAnd even as they wilt before the heat of the fireball, the plants realize that it was the Plantman who got them into this mess, and attack <i>him</i> instead, destroying the very device with which he was controlling them.&nbsp; The villain's entire plot to rule the universe has been thwarted!&nbsp; (But in the process, Plantman himself avoids capture by hiding in a nearby hollow tree trunk, vowing to rebuild his device and one day return to destroy the Human Torch!)\n\nDoris thanks the Torch for clearing her father's name but still has no interest in him \"flaming on all the time!\"&nbsp; Johnny feels as if he just can't win...",
  "grade_one_liner": "A mock-worthy misfire you endure just to bask in the botanical derangement.\nThe lone bright spot is Johnny's girlfriend, who wants nothing to do with his flame.",
  "subtitle": "Johnny's New \"Flame\" (who hates flames)",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | MeetDorrieEvans | width=12%] The novelty of the Human Torch having a girlfriend (Doris Evans) who is completely against him using his flame gets the story off on an interesting note.&nbsp; Even Johnny seems taken aback at how <i>little</i> interest she has in him parading around in what she calls a \"freakish fiery get-up.\"&nbsp; (For once, the Human Torch's biggest fan isn't the girl on his arm.)\n[BR]\n[BR] While we can scratch our heads and wonder how she managed to get a date with Johnny Storm in the first place without somehow realizing [SPAN: severity=warning; text=the full weight of what that <i>implied</i>], it was still funny to see Johnny reduced to catching a bus -- the <i>least</i> glamorous mode of transportation imaginable for a member of the Fantastic Four.\n\n[ASSET: left | CombinationLock | width=12%] As for the Plantman, if the \"one-in-a-million freak accident\" wasn't bad enough, the IQ-enhancing device that allows him to control plant life is <i>so good</i> that it allows a plant to actually <b>remember the combination</b> of a safe inside a jewelry store, the one managed by the father of Doris Evans, who [SPAN: severity=warning; text=coincidentally enough], had just been fired in front of Johnny Storm when he went to pick up Doris for their date.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Stan really wasn't shy about stacking coincidences like cordwood this month.)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I ask you... what are the odds <i>against</i> this?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=Astronomical doesn't even <i>begin</i> to cover it...]\n\n[ASSET: right | Confession | width=18%] And when the Plantman is finally forced into a confrontation with the Human Torch, he [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=immediately confesses] and admits that he framed Evans.\n[BR]\n[BR] This is what you confess only <i>after</i> someone has been thoroughly defeated -- not before the fight even <i>starts</i>.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=What an idiot!]\n\nAlso note the narration on this panel, which says [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=\"the\"] jewelry store, not [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=\"a\"] jewelry store.&nbsp; This only <i>reinforces</i> that the guy is an idiot.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Why return to the same jewelry store after you've already robbed it and successfully implicated Evans?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(The whole notion is so <i>dumb</i>, that I'm choosing to believe this was simply a misprint.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nEven after avoiding capture by the Human Torch (by surprising him with his abilities), the now-costumed Plantman shows up at Central Park and orders everyone there to clear out, even ordering a policeman to notify the mayor to \"surrender the city to me at once\" or else his plants will be ordered to \"tear down City Hall!\"&nbsp; People flee in panic!\n[ASSET: center | Pandemonium | width=67%]\n\nBut even though the Plantman is <i>correct</i> that plants vastly outnumber the \"puny humans\" in the city, and indeed on the entire <i>planet</i>, is the conquest of Earth good enough for him?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Heck no!]&nbsp; \"Today, Earth!&nbsp; Tomorrow, <i>the stars</i>!!!\"\n[ASSET: center | TomorrowTheStars | width=67%]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I'm sorry, Stan, but this is where I'm forced to ask if that lightning bolt didn't strike <i>you</i> instead of Sam!&nbsp; Are you <i>kidding </i>me?)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ConvenientVatOfWater | width=12%] The final confrontation between Plantman and the Human Torch in Central Park did at least make <i>some sense</i> as a locale, as it afforded the Plantman plenty of obedient servants to hurl missiles at Johnny (in the form of acorns and/or fruit), but when he went to tip over a vat of water... that was simply a bridge too far.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Seriously?]&nbsp; Vats of water are simply positioned around Central Park?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<i>Sure</i>, why <b>not</b>?&nbsp; Maybe they're right next to the conveniently placed barrels of rocket fuel!)]\n\n[ASSET: right | DeviceDestroyed | width=15%] Johnny's giant fireball that begins to wilt the plants around him was a decent counterattack, but when the plants realize that it was the Plantman himself who got them into this mess, they lash out and destroy the very device that gave them \"superior intelligence.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>Presto</i>!&nbsp; Scratch one more brilliant plan for \"ruling the universe!\"&nbsp; [SPAN; fontsize=small; text=(It's hard to rule the cosmos when your own houseplants stage a mutiny.)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=Thank goodness!]&nbsp; <i>What a close shave that was!</i>\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt was downright astonishing that this \"Plantman\" character was ever put forth as \"one of the most dangerous foes you've ever encountered\" (quoted from page 2).&nbsp; He was nothing more than a complete [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=joke!]\n\nAs a villain, the Plantman is lame <i>enough</i>, but even so, I could at least swallow him seizing an opportunity to turn to crime and rob a store using his newfound abilities to control the plants inside (even as my eyebrows rose at the notion of the remembered combination).&nbsp; I could even stomach the convenience of him turning out to be the \"Sam\" that Johnny overheard being fired by Doris's father (as this is a comic book after all, and that is simply par for the course).\n\nBut the notion of:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=\"Today, Earth!&nbsp; Tomorrow, <i>the stars!!!</i>\"]\n\nIt's as if Stan got his [SPAN: severity=error; text=tang tongled -- and yes, I mean <b>tang tongled</b> --] when he went over his plot with his scripter,\n[BR] who must have thought [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=\"<i>You can't be <b>serious</b>!\"</i>]\n\nThe Plantman was such an aggressively unremarkable villain whose crazed mind actually went straight from robbing a single jewelry store to the conquest of the <i>stars</i> within five pages, the very definition of a deranged megalomaniac.&nbsp; And then to see the plants strike out at their master in the end?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Argh!]&nbsp; What seems even <i>worse</i>, though, is that this idiot gets <i>away</i> at the end, which only threatens a repeat appearance.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Dear god, <b>no</b>!)]\n\nAll this being said, the novelty of the Human Torch having a girlfriend who is <i>completely</i> against him using his flame is the issue's only salvation, and the one actually intriguing idea to be found.&nbsp; The irony is very much like what we saw last month in <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i>, where the students of Midtown High School were shown rooting for Spider-Man while they revile Peter Parker every chance they get.\n\nWhat will the Thing say when he hears about this?\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=(You can practically hear him roaring with laughter already.)]\n\nThe thought is admittedly funny, but otherwise, this one was a chore to read and has <i>zero</i> chance of even approaching a reader grade in the end.",
  "decoder_ring": "I'm very disappointed that it appears we'll have to endure another appearance from him at some point since he got away.&nbsp; According to the comic book database, his name is Sam Smithers, and unfortunately, it appears we'll be seeing him again multiple times.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Sigh!]",
  "highlight": "The Human Torch battles the unhinged Plantman and meets a new \"flame\" who wants him extinguished."
}